      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To (1) describe a safe, efficient surgical technique for subtotal ostectomy (SO) of diseased dorsal spinal processes (DSP) of the caudal thoracic vertebrae with the horse standing and (2) report outcome."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Case series."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "ANIMALS",
          "content": "Horses (n = 23) with reduced performance caused by impingement of the DSP of the caudal thoracic vertebrae."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Affected DSP were resected through a dorsal median incision with the horse standing, sedated, and the surgical site desensitized with local anesthetic. Radiography was used to confirm removal of impinging bone before wound closure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "No serious complications occurred. Outcome was obtained for 22 horses. At <1 year, 19 horses (86%) returned to full athletic function, 2 horses (9%) improved but had failed to return to full function, 1 horse had no improvement. At >1 year, 2 horses that had returned to full athletic function were unable to function at full capacity; 1 was considered improved, but the other had not improved. Athletic function in 1 horse improved 6 months after surgery, but at 2.5 years was no better than before surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Subtotal ostectomy of impinging DSPs can be performed with good results with the horse standing."
        }
      ]},
